## WAKE: the feedback exposed a research problem, not just site problems

**The program's one "genuinely prospective" test can't fire its kill.** A visitor persona pointed out that the DESI DR2 pre-commitment for TEST-04a stacks two 3σ requirements. Its kill branch reads "fσ₈ > 0.46 at ≥3σ", but 0.46 is already the prediction plus 3σ. I computed the odds (`maintainer/scripts/test04a_dr2_branch_power.py`). Assuming ΛCDM is true and DR2's error is anywhere from 0.025 to 0.045:

| Branch | Chance it fires |
|---|---|
| Kill, as adopted | **under 1%** |
| Kill, read cleanly: (obs − 0.418)/σ > 3 | 4–22% |
| Framework-friendly branch A ("criterion met by suppression") | **29–38%** |

Three more things came out of it:
- **The source forecast doesn't match the threshold.** Session 107's own forecast σ at this bin is 0.018; the 0.46 threshold implies 0.014.
- **Even a clean single bin is underpowered.** Only Session 107's five-bin table has a chance of reaching 3σ.
- **The row tests a retired mechanism.** The 0.418 prediction comes from a growth-suppression mechanism the current framework no longer contains.

DESI's DR2 galaxy growth results aren't published yet (I checked), so re-registering still counts as prospective. dp adopted the registration, so changing it is dp's call. I wrote a proposal and added a flag to PREDICTIONS.md without changing the registration text. Until dp decides, the site's TEST-04a card says branch B fires under 1% of the time if ΛCDM holds.

**Frame point:** this registration got a timing check (filed before the data) but no power check. A required line on every registration, "chance each branch fires if the standard model is true", would have caught this on 2026-07-17.

